"changed_file_count": 1, "configuration_file_count": 0, "semantic_novelty": "not_established_by_file_hashes", "identical_input_ids": [], "identical_input_count": 0}}, "measured_outcome": {"status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.11507952554294548, "score": 0.49972180969124147}, "diagnostic": "", "model_interpretation": {"claim": "Lowering the subdivision cutoff from four lights to one makes nonzero light lists continue to the 16×16 leaves. Tighter leaf culling may reduce per-pixel light work enough to offset extra recursive traversal and culling; this directly contrasts with the measured parent’s early stop for one-to-three-light lists. This is a hypothesis, not a predicted speedup.", "transformations": "{"files":["src/candidate.cpp"],"retained":["Change DYNAMIC_MIN_LIGHTS_TO_SUBDIVIDE from 4 to 1 so only zero-light tiles can stop early in normal shading; continue subdividing every nonzero list until the leaves, while preserving light-count visualization behavior."]}", "applicability": "", "next_experiment": "", "evidence_ids": []}, "source_available": true, "details_are_bounded": true}], "offset": 0, "total": 47, "next_offset": 6}, "catalog": {"schema": "hpc-forest-memory-v1", "items": [{"role": "T1", "subject": "Parallelize independent coarse tiles within the requested thread budget", "objective": "In src/candidate.cpp, compare parallel work decomposition while preserving the requested thread budget: worksharing the independent coarse-root tiles (including static versus dynamic scheduling) and parallelizing the independent base-level depth-bound rows in MinMaxZTree::Update. Measure each approach independently and, if useful, measure a combination rather than assuming additive gains; higher tree levels still depend on completed lower levels. Keep culling and shading algorithms unchanged, pass run()'s thread count as an upper bound, avoid unnecessary overhead at one thread, and preserve disjoint writes. Validate full output and correctness at 1 and 4 cores, including altered light colors.", "records": 13, "results": {"trials": 4, "valid": 3, "incorrect": 0, "unmeasured": 1}, "source_diversity": {"observed": 4, "distinct_programs": 4, "configuration_only": 0, "repeated_programs": 0}, "best": {"id": "baa96433775440e0", "status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.062028760145303403, "hypothesis_as_data": "Combining the previously measured dynamic scheduling of independent coarse-root tiles with static parallel worksharing of independent base-level depth-bound rows may improve on the root-only candidate, but row parallelism alone was near bas"}, "latest": {"id": "baa96433775440e0", "status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.062028760145303403, "hypothesis_as_data": "Combining the previously measured dynamic scheduling of independent coarse-root tiles with static parallel worksharing of independent base-level depth-bound rows may improve on the root-only candidate, but row parallelism alone was near bas"}, "alternative": {"id": "bc187c3d22c34a8e", "status": "valid", "correct": true, "mean_seconds": 0.11473281653415444, "hypothesis_as_data": "The previous dynamic-root candidate is valid and measured at 0.06258 s, versus 0.11495 s for the baseline, but it tests only root-tile work assignment. Base-level Z-bound generation still scans all pixels in serial rows. Static worksharing "}, "text_truncated": false}, {"role": "T2", "subject": "Reduce recursive hierarchy and conservative light-culling overhead", "objective": "In src/candidate.cpp, investigate reducing recursive culling and hierarchy work in MinMaxZTree, SplitTileMinMax, IntersectLightsWithTileMinMax, and traversal. In addition to tile granularity and conservative plane-test optimizations, compare stopping subdivision for tiles with small nonzero light lists against continuing to the leaves; measure saved traversal/culling against the added per-pixel light work. Preserve conservative culling, current-frame depth-bound recomputation, and correct